  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Subtropical Storm Josephine, continues to accelerate\nnortheastward away from our area. As expected a marked decrease\nin winds have occurred with seas still running quite high off\nour coast, but lower than previous days. With continued large \nsurf and longer period swell, coastal flooding impacts along \nvulnerable locations of the coast will continue through early\ntonight, especially along Hatteras Island where dune structure \nis fragile. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. The next high \ntides are around 530AM Friday. A Coastal Flood Warning remains \nin effect for Hatteras Island and a Coastal Flood Advisory for \nthe Northern Outer Banks. Additionally, a high surf advisory \ncontinues for the beaches from Duck to Cape Hatteras where surf \nheights 8-12 ft will through early evening. Beyond tonight, \ncontinued lowering trend in waves and swell will gradually give \nus a lull Friday before conditions pick up again (see key \nmessage 3 below). It should be noted that although water levels \nmay fall below typical values that would cause flooding, due to \nthe extensive erosion and dune loss, impacts Friday may still \ncontinue. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Made some slight tweaks to the dewpoint and\ntemperature forecast for tonight given previous days cold\nweather. Expect dewpoints to range from the mid to upper 30s\ntonight and with winds potentially decoupling again late\ntonight, we could see temps lower into the mid to upper 30s as \nwell especially across the more sheltered locations such as \nWallace or Kenansville. This in turn could bring a patchy frost \nthreat to portions of the Coastal Plain tonight, though a big \ncaveat in this threat is that it may be too dry for frost \nformation even with the cold air in place. Otherwise the rest of\nthe forecast remains on track with dry weather and clear skies \nforecast this evening across ENC. \n\nPleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us\nthrough the next several days with temps a bit below climo. \nMaximum temperatures through the period are expected to remain \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s range. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the 40s inland to the 50s coast. Good radiational cooling\nconditions are expected again tonight. With afternoon dewpoints\nin the upper 30s, lows in our typical cold spots should drop\ninto the 30s once again. Model soundings do show just a few more\nknots of wind in the boundary layer compared to yesterday, so we\nmay not be quite as cold as last night.  A bit more mixing \nensues Friday night as strong high pressure builds in from the \nnorth preventing temps from cratering. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong high pressure building in from the north\nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period\nof breezy to strong NE winds returning late Friday through the\nweekend with strongest winds along the coast, especially south\nof Cape Hatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, \ncombined with developing King Tides could lead to additional \nminor coastal impacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).",
